WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.942 --> 00:00:10.082
This is my favorite subject.

00:00:10.082 --> 00:00:13.172
I spent 15 years
of my life building

00:00:13.172 --> 00:00:16.902
UIs in the traditional
event handler way.

00:00:16.902 --> 00:00:23.747
It just felt so, so hard that
it was almost offensive to me,

00:00:23.747 --> 00:00:28.321
that I should need to use every
single ounce of my brainpower

00:00:28.321 --> 00:00:32.462
to hold in my head this pretty
small dialogue in a Windows

00:00:32.462 --> 00:00:33.822
app.

00:00:33.822 --> 00:00:36.122
It's not the most complicated
thing in the world.

00:00:36.122 --> 00:00:41.852
And yet I am straining
all of my brain cells

00:00:41.852 --> 00:00:45.042
to make sure that all these
relationships are correct.

00:00:45.042 --> 00:00:48.272
It seems like everything you
add makes it exponentially more

00:00:48.272 --> 00:00:48.842
complicated.

00:00:48.842 --> 00:00:50.042
That's exactly right.

00:00:50.042 --> 00:00:53.892
All nontrivial software, this
is the number one problem,

00:00:53.892 --> 00:00:57.692
how do we manage the complexity,
because everything we add

00:00:57.692 --> 00:01:00.462
has the potential to interact
with everything else.

00:01:00.462 --> 00:01:03.782
So what I want to say about
this reactive graph issue,

00:01:03.782 --> 00:01:06.302
where you start out with
an input and an output,

00:01:06.302 --> 00:01:08.282
not even a reactive
in the middle.

00:01:08.282 --> 00:01:11.972
Just input, output, multiple
inputs to one output,

00:01:11.972 --> 00:01:14.172
multiple outputs to one input.

00:01:14.172 --> 00:01:14.672
Sure.

00:01:14.672 --> 00:01:16.862
All of that is pretty
understandable.

00:01:16.862 --> 00:01:20.762
And then you add one or
two reactive calculations

00:01:20.762 --> 00:01:21.422
in the middle.

00:01:21.422 --> 00:01:23.222
But it doesn't
take much for that

00:01:23.222 --> 00:01:27.092
to start becoming
very crazy-looking,

00:01:27.092 --> 00:01:30.152
especially when you
have multiple levels

00:01:30.152 --> 00:01:31.802
of reactive calculations.

00:01:31.802 --> 00:01:34.262
I think the most important
thing to understand

00:01:34.262 --> 00:01:37.382
is that you're not supposed
to understand the whole graph.

00:01:37.382 --> 00:01:40.142
The goal is not to create
a graph that you look at

00:01:40.142 --> 00:01:42.452
and say, oh, that's
definitely right.

00:01:42.452 --> 00:01:45.422
Think of this as
the same exact thing

00:01:45.422 --> 00:01:48.182
with functions in a
normal piece of software

00:01:48.182 --> 00:01:49.502
or a normal package.

00:01:49.502 --> 00:01:51.902
Which functions call
which other functions?

00:01:51.902 --> 00:01:53.372
And you can draw
a graph with that.

00:01:53.372 --> 00:01:55.172
You can draw a function
[? called ?] graph.

00:01:55.172 --> 00:01:56.252
And people do.

00:01:56.252 --> 00:01:58.072
There's software that
does that for you.

00:01:58.072 --> 00:01:59.612
It helps you make
those connections.

00:01:59.612 --> 00:02:02.282
The goal is never
to be able to look

00:02:02.282 --> 00:02:06.182
at that entire
graph of functions

00:02:06.182 --> 00:02:10.832
pointing at each other and
be like, oh, yep, it's right.

00:02:10.832 --> 00:02:11.862
That's not the point.

00:02:11.862 --> 00:02:13.952
If your software
is well-written,

00:02:13.952 --> 00:02:19.142
the point is to be able to look
at any one node in that graph

00:02:19.142 --> 00:02:22.952
and prove to yourself
that that node is correct,

00:02:22.952 --> 00:02:26.042
to look at one
output and just look

00:02:26.042 --> 00:02:30.482
at the things that are calling
in to that output, just one

00:02:30.482 --> 00:02:31.562
level.

00:02:31.562 --> 00:02:33.752
Don't go beyond the arrows
that are going directly

00:02:33.752 --> 00:02:38.192
into that output and
say, are those correct?

00:02:38.192 --> 00:02:41.342
And if the answer is yes,
then you can move on.

00:02:41.342 --> 00:02:44.552
You can move on to look
at each of the other nodes

00:02:44.552 --> 00:02:46.332
in the graph.

00:02:46.332 --> 00:02:50.162
So the point is not to be
able to reason globally.

00:02:50.162 --> 00:02:53.102
The point is to be
able to reason locally.

00:02:53.102 --> 00:02:58.052
This is exactly the same thing
that we say about functions.

00:02:58.052 --> 00:03:00.782
When you write a
function, the goal

00:03:00.782 --> 00:03:04.922
is not to be able to take the
entire rest of your code base

00:03:04.922 --> 00:03:08.522
that might indirectly
or directly call

00:03:08.522 --> 00:03:11.252
or be called by a function
that's under the question.

00:03:11.252 --> 00:03:14.222
The goal is to be able
to look at this function

00:03:14.222 --> 00:03:18.542
and to be able to confidently
say if the functions that this

00:03:18.542 --> 00:03:22.404
calls are written correctly,
then this logic is correct.

00:03:22.404 --> 00:03:24.362
So you need to be able
to look at this function

00:03:24.362 --> 00:03:28.122
and reason about just this
function in isolation.

00:03:28.122 --> 00:03:29.702
Now, you might be wrong.

00:03:29.702 --> 00:03:31.982
You might be assuming that
those other functions are

00:03:31.982 --> 00:03:33.002
written correctly.

00:03:33.002 --> 00:03:34.532
And that might not be true.

00:03:34.532 --> 00:03:37.562
But that problem is
a much better problem

00:03:37.562 --> 00:03:39.872
to have to tackle
than the possibility

00:03:39.872 --> 00:03:42.602
that all those functions
are correctly written,

00:03:42.602 --> 00:03:45.345
and you still don't know
if your code is correct,

00:03:45.345 --> 00:03:47.762
because not only do you have
to know that they're correct,

00:03:47.762 --> 00:03:50.402
but you have to know about
really subtle interactions

00:03:50.402 --> 00:03:52.232
that those functions
have with each other.

00:03:52.232 --> 00:03:57.902
With reactivity, this is a way
that same quality of, I can

00:03:57.902 --> 00:04:00.212
look at a small piece of code.

00:04:00.212 --> 00:04:04.412
And just by knowing
that the reactives that

00:04:04.412 --> 00:04:06.452
are being called--

00:04:06.452 --> 00:04:10.052
just assume that those are going
to execute at the right time.

00:04:10.052 --> 00:04:12.151
Then this is going to
execute at the right time.

00:04:12.151 --> 00:04:14.072
If there is something
that I'm calling

00:04:14.072 --> 00:04:16.382
and I don't want to
update when it updates,

00:04:16.382 --> 00:04:19.752
then I need to know to
call isolate around that.

00:04:19.752 --> 00:04:21.482
So not calling
isolate when I don't

00:04:21.482 --> 00:04:23.852
want a reactive
relationship, that

00:04:23.852 --> 00:04:26.132
would be a bug in this code.

00:04:26.132 --> 00:04:31.202
But if I don't have that
bug, if my relationship

00:04:31.202 --> 00:04:35.642
with my immediate things
that I depend on is correct,

00:04:35.642 --> 00:04:38.315
then I'm satisfied that this
piece of code is correct.

00:04:38.315 --> 00:04:40.232
And now I can go look
at another piece of code

00:04:40.232 --> 00:04:42.452
and make sure that it's correct.

00:04:42.452 --> 00:04:46.202
The bottom line being if you
have an app that's written out

00:04:46.202 --> 00:04:50.042
of 10 such pieces
of code, then you

00:04:50.042 --> 00:04:52.842
have to make sure that 10
pieces of code are correct.

00:04:52.842 --> 00:04:54.537
And if you add an
11th piece of code,

00:04:54.537 --> 00:04:56.412
you have to make sure
that 11th piece of code

00:04:56.412 --> 00:04:59.712
is correct as opposed
to having to make sure

00:04:59.712 --> 00:05:03.042
that each piece of code and all
of the possible interactions it

00:05:03.042 --> 00:05:06.222
could ever have with any
of the rest of the pieces.

00:05:06.222 --> 00:05:08.172
Now, that's not 10
things you need to check.

00:05:08.172 --> 00:05:10.782
It's 10 factorial or
something like that.

00:05:10.782 --> 00:05:13.782
Or I don't know
what the math is.

00:05:13.782 --> 00:05:15.672
But it is something
that's certainly

00:05:15.672 --> 00:05:18.942
nonlinear in terms of each
additional thing you add.

00:05:18.942 --> 00:05:21.942
You now have to check
all the possible things

00:05:21.942 --> 00:05:23.472
that it could interact with.

00:05:23.472 --> 00:05:24.042
Oh, my gosh.

00:05:24.042 --> 00:05:25.092
I'm going to add a new thing.

00:05:25.092 --> 00:05:26.562
And it's going to
be so complicated

00:05:26.562 --> 00:05:29.712
to know whether this is
going to blow up something

00:05:29.712 --> 00:05:31.842
that I already have.

00:05:31.842 --> 00:05:34.332
That was the world
that we lived in

00:05:34.332 --> 00:05:36.012
before we reactivity
when it came

00:05:36.012 --> 00:05:37.915
to writing interactive UIs.

00:05:37.915 --> 00:05:38.832
This might go nowhere.

00:05:38.832 --> 00:05:41.622
But I think when people think
about the LEGO analogy, which

00:05:41.622 --> 00:05:43.152
that comes up a
lot when you talk

00:05:43.152 --> 00:05:44.772
about components and software.

00:05:44.772 --> 00:05:47.772
A lot of people think
of them as LEGOs.

00:05:47.772 --> 00:05:49.692
At a high level, you
tend to think, oh,

00:05:49.692 --> 00:05:52.480
I want the ability
to snap anything.

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:54.522
Anything should be able
to snap to anything else.

00:05:54.522 --> 00:05:55.872
I just want to take
two software components

00:05:55.872 --> 00:05:56.862
and snap them together.

00:05:56.862 --> 00:05:58.962
I don't get that much
pushback on reactivity.

00:05:58.962 --> 00:06:01.135
Like, oh, why didn't you
do reactivity this way?

00:06:01.135 --> 00:06:02.802
There just has not
been that much of it.

00:06:02.802 --> 00:06:05.832
I think because it's
sufficiently complicated

00:06:05.832 --> 00:06:10.422
that it's not the most
natural thing to bikeshed on.

00:06:10.422 --> 00:06:13.062
But modules are the opposite.

00:06:13.062 --> 00:06:15.522
Everybody has got an
opinion on modules.

00:06:15.522 --> 00:06:19.362
And I mean, for most
of the life of Shiny,

00:06:19.362 --> 00:06:24.852
people have had opinions on why
I didn't do it the right way,

00:06:24.852 --> 00:06:26.502
and the right way
meaning something

00:06:26.502 --> 00:06:29.412
so different to every person.

00:06:29.412 --> 00:06:33.642
But one really common
refrain was, basically,

00:06:33.642 --> 00:06:36.222
people don't like that
you have to pass in.

00:06:36.222 --> 00:06:39.012
If you have something
like a module

00:06:39.012 --> 00:06:40.602
that takes some
kind of input that

00:06:40.602 --> 00:06:44.802
needs to be provided from
somewhere else on the app--

00:06:44.802 --> 00:06:47.982
you've got a module that does
something with a data frame.

00:06:47.982 --> 00:06:49.602
But it doesn't
itself want to have

00:06:49.602 --> 00:06:54.192
any opinion about what that data
frame is or where it came from

00:06:54.192 --> 00:06:55.932
or how it specified.

00:06:55.932 --> 00:06:59.842
It wants to specialize just
on some output or whatever.

00:06:59.842 --> 00:07:02.112
How do you communicate
with that module?

00:07:02.112 --> 00:07:05.772
How do you tell it this is
the data frame you should use

00:07:05.772 --> 00:07:07.692
and here's when it changes?

00:07:07.692 --> 00:07:10.932
And the way that
works in Shiny modules

00:07:10.932 --> 00:07:13.812
is when you define
the module, you

00:07:13.812 --> 00:07:16.962
take that data
frame as an input.

00:07:16.962 --> 00:07:20.862
You take a reactive
calculation as an input.

00:07:20.862 --> 00:07:22.882
A lot of people
push back on that.

00:07:22.882 --> 00:07:27.612
And they're like, I don't
want to have all these things

00:07:27.612 --> 00:07:31.062
that I'm passing
into this LEGO brick.

00:07:31.062 --> 00:07:34.602
Why can't all LEGO bricks
just look the same?

00:07:34.602 --> 00:07:39.282
I don't want to pass in these
four arguments that it needs.

00:07:39.282 --> 00:07:43.212
I just want to give
it a dictionary.

00:07:43.212 --> 00:07:44.082
How about that?

00:07:44.082 --> 00:07:46.062
Every module just
takes a dictionary.

00:07:46.062 --> 00:07:47.979
And then you just have
to put the right things

00:07:47.979 --> 00:07:51.072
in the dictionary
and then look how

00:07:51.072 --> 00:07:53.532
clean the code is for snapping
together all these LEGOs.

00:07:53.532 --> 00:07:55.812
I just make a dictionary,
put some stuff in it,

00:07:55.812 --> 00:07:58.362
and then everybody
gets the dictionary.

00:07:58.362 --> 00:08:00.852
And, oh, I can
use the dictionary

00:08:00.852 --> 00:08:02.142
for both input and output.

00:08:02.142 --> 00:08:04.362
Not only can I get
data frames out

00:08:04.362 --> 00:08:09.402
of it or other reactive things,
I can also push values into it.

00:08:09.402 --> 00:08:11.202
And then other modules
can consume it.

00:08:11.202 --> 00:08:15.702
And look how easy it is to
snap these LEGOs together.

00:08:15.702 --> 00:08:19.872
This is where it's not enough
that the LEGOs go together.

00:08:19.872 --> 00:08:22.452
They have to go
together correctly.

00:08:22.452 --> 00:08:27.252
So LEGO minifigs have
heads and torsos and legs.

00:08:27.252 --> 00:08:30.191
You can't put a head
on the legs directly.

00:08:30.191 --> 00:08:34.212
They only connect one way.

00:08:34.212 --> 00:08:36.671
And those connections
are obvious.

00:08:36.671 --> 00:08:40.582
With a lot of components,
it's actually more like that.

00:08:40.582 --> 00:08:43.362
You don't want LEGOs that will
snap together, whether they

00:08:43.362 --> 00:08:45.012
belong together or not.

00:08:45.012 --> 00:08:46.932
You actually want it
to be very obvious

00:08:46.932 --> 00:08:49.362
that this thing needs to
connect with something

00:08:49.362 --> 00:08:50.122
that's like this.

00:08:50.122 --> 00:08:51.622
It's more like puzzle pieces.

00:08:51.622 --> 00:08:54.972
So people arguing
for, oh, let me just

00:08:54.972 --> 00:08:57.912
have a dictionary that I pass
in, what they're really saying

00:08:57.912 --> 00:09:00.642
is like, oh, it's so
annoying putting together

00:09:00.642 --> 00:09:03.342
this puzzle with all the
pieces are all jagged.

00:09:03.342 --> 00:09:06.672
Wouldn't it be easier if
they were all just squares?

00:09:06.672 --> 00:09:09.342
Let me put together
this 500-piece puzzle.

00:09:09.342 --> 00:09:13.697
But it'll be so much easier
if they're all just squares.

00:09:13.697 --> 00:09:15.072
You're actually
making it so much

00:09:15.072 --> 00:09:20.082
harder because that
problem still exists.

00:09:20.082 --> 00:09:25.062
You still need to make sure that
that data frame that you need

00:09:25.062 --> 00:09:27.282
is provided to you.

00:09:27.282 --> 00:09:29.442
It's just now provided
in a very hidden way

00:09:29.442 --> 00:09:31.032
that you have to go speak out.

00:09:31.032 --> 00:09:32.352
That's not obvious.

00:09:32.352 --> 00:09:35.652
So you've removed the signal
of how these things connect.

00:09:35.652 --> 00:09:38.172
You've removed these sort
of weirdly shaped borders

00:09:38.172 --> 00:09:40.572
and replace them
with flat borders,

00:09:40.572 --> 00:09:42.852
even though they can
really still only connect

00:09:42.852 --> 00:09:45.232
in one way that makes sense.

00:09:45.232 --> 00:09:48.402
So, again, just another
example of that principle

00:09:48.402 --> 00:09:53.562
of small pieces that we can
locally reason about and then

00:09:53.562 --> 00:09:55.922
combine them in reliable ways.

00:09:55.922 --> 00:09:57.812
And when we have just
like these dictionaries

00:09:57.812 --> 00:10:00.932
that we pass around, it's
removing that part of,

00:10:00.932 --> 00:10:02.972
we need to be able to
combine these reliably.

00:10:06.192 --> 00:10:10.052
There's so many different ways
I want to answer this question.

00:10:10.052 --> 00:10:12.572
Certainly, between building
the front end and the back end,

00:10:12.572 --> 00:10:14.072
I don't think there's
a right order.

00:10:14.072 --> 00:10:17.822
If what is going to get you
excited about the project

00:10:17.822 --> 00:10:22.622
enough to build that momentum
is that you have this cool UI

00:10:22.622 --> 00:10:25.472
concept in mind or
that just motivates you

00:10:25.472 --> 00:10:28.382
to look at something concrete
and see it sort of one piece

00:10:28.382 --> 00:10:30.812
after another light
up, absolutely

00:10:30.812 --> 00:10:33.212
go crazy with the UI first.

00:10:33.212 --> 00:10:36.542
I don't think there's
anything wrong with that.

00:10:36.542 --> 00:10:42.242
If, instead, you really
have some kind of R code

00:10:42.242 --> 00:10:46.442
on the server that you're really
excited to see come to life

00:10:46.442 --> 00:10:48.002
for the first time
and that is going

00:10:48.002 --> 00:10:49.592
to be what excites
you and propels you

00:10:49.592 --> 00:10:52.022
through the rest
of the project, I

00:10:52.022 --> 00:10:54.542
don't think that there
is a wrong way to do it.

00:10:54.542 --> 00:10:57.452
If you want to end
up with this a bunch

00:10:57.452 --> 00:11:01.442
of well-named, well-reasoned
nicely factored reactives

00:11:01.442 --> 00:11:04.382
and outputs and whatever,
is that something you

00:11:04.382 --> 00:11:08.342
do on a whiteboard first
and then stub each one out

00:11:08.342 --> 00:11:09.782
and make it so?

00:11:09.782 --> 00:11:12.932
Or is it more something
that you just start coding

00:11:12.932 --> 00:11:14.882
and then extract out the
things that you need?

00:11:14.882 --> 00:11:16.965
Again, I don't think there's
a wrong way to do it.

00:11:16.965 --> 00:11:20.222
But I would draw
a direct analogy

00:11:20.222 --> 00:11:24.032
to when you're writing
either a package or maybe

00:11:24.032 --> 00:11:25.232
a complicated script.

00:11:25.232 --> 00:11:27.542
When and how do you
introduce functions?

00:11:27.542 --> 00:11:30.092
Because it's exactly
the same process that

00:11:30.092 --> 00:11:32.882
applies here as well, I think.

00:11:32.882 --> 00:11:36.242
Maybe it's a little bit more
urgent because it's not going

00:11:36.242 --> 00:11:38.522
to react unless you find
somewhere to park this code

00:11:38.522 --> 00:11:42.742
that is [? in ?] [? reactive. ?]
So in that sense,

00:11:42.742 --> 00:11:44.492
you are going to be
forced to confront it.

00:11:44.492 --> 00:11:49.874
But some people might write
straight-line code that's--

00:11:49.874 --> 00:11:51.582
I'm just going to get
this thing working.

00:11:51.582 --> 00:11:53.040
I'm going to get
the answer I want.

00:11:53.040 --> 00:11:54.722
And then let me take
a step back and see

00:11:54.722 --> 00:11:56.762
where are opportunities
to refactor

00:11:56.762 --> 00:11:58.412
and turn into functions.

00:11:58.412 --> 00:12:00.542
I think that's probably
the most common.

00:12:00.542 --> 00:12:04.892
And for that reason,
you might go a long time

00:12:04.892 --> 00:12:07.172
without ever learning
how to write functions

00:12:07.172 --> 00:12:10.562
if your job is more about
finding that answer the one

00:12:10.562 --> 00:12:11.942
time and then moving on.

00:12:11.942 --> 00:12:15.482
So I would call that sort
of a bottom-up approach.

00:12:15.482 --> 00:12:17.252
You've got the logic,
and then now you're

00:12:17.252 --> 00:12:19.562
trying to extract
structure out of it.

00:12:19.562 --> 00:12:23.252
I think that's probably the
more common way people think,

00:12:23.252 --> 00:12:24.542
their brains work.

00:12:24.542 --> 00:12:26.222
I happen to be the other way.

00:12:26.222 --> 00:12:29.522
Whereas I will start with--
if I could wave a magic wand

00:12:29.522 --> 00:12:31.862
and have whatever
functions I need

00:12:31.862 --> 00:12:36.062
to exist if they could exist,
what little piece of code

00:12:36.062 --> 00:12:39.162
would I write that would solve
the problem that I have here?

00:12:39.162 --> 00:12:43.022
So for some really simple
example, I might say I want--

00:12:43.022 --> 00:12:46.422
I'll wave my magic wand
and say load data set,

00:12:46.422 --> 00:12:47.762
remove outliers, and clean.

00:12:47.762 --> 00:12:49.652
Maybe that's another function.

00:12:49.652 --> 00:12:55.802
And then let me generate a
set of outputs using these two

00:12:55.802 --> 00:12:57.932
functions that I wish existed.

00:12:57.932 --> 00:13:00.822
And I will just call them
even though they don't exist,

00:13:00.822 --> 00:13:02.072
and then I will stub them out.

00:13:02.072 --> 00:13:04.862
So that's working
exactly the opposite way.

00:13:04.862 --> 00:13:06.722
I'm calling functions
that don't exist.

00:13:06.722 --> 00:13:09.085
And then each of those
functions is potentially

00:13:09.085 --> 00:13:10.502
going to call
functions that don't

00:13:10.502 --> 00:13:12.182
exist until,
eventually, they start

00:13:12.182 --> 00:13:14.882
calling functions that exist.

00:13:14.882 --> 00:13:19.112
And then you basically create
a skeleton for the structure

00:13:19.112 --> 00:13:23.642
first and then fill out, go back
and fill out all the pieces.

00:13:23.642 --> 00:13:28.472
If you can get to this place
either way of small components

00:13:28.472 --> 00:13:31.742
that you can individually
reason about and combine them

00:13:31.742 --> 00:13:35.942
reliably, that's
ultimately what matters.

00:13:35.942 --> 00:13:38.192
So, yeah, in this second--

00:13:38.192 --> 00:13:41.642
the top down way where you
call the function that you wish

00:13:41.642 --> 00:13:46.952
existed, making sure that that
they're functions that you

00:13:46.952 --> 00:13:49.852
want to be, well, functional.

00:13:49.852 --> 00:13:53.012
You tend to want them not
to use global state if you

00:13:53.012 --> 00:13:54.122
can help it.

00:13:54.122 --> 00:13:55.802
I guess in the
Python world, there

00:13:55.802 --> 00:13:57.662
might be classes that
you wish existed.

00:13:57.662 --> 00:14:00.132
You [? knew ?] [? of ?] an
object that doesn't exist yet.

00:14:00.132 --> 00:14:02.582
You call methods
that don't exist.

00:14:02.582 --> 00:14:05.012
And then you can go back
and fill those things out.

00:14:05.012 --> 00:14:10.082
It is harder to do the top down
if it's an unfamiliar domain.

00:14:10.082 --> 00:14:13.292
I think the more speculative
and the more like, oh,

00:14:13.292 --> 00:14:15.122
my gosh, how is this
even going to work,

00:14:15.122 --> 00:14:18.462
the harder it is to go
that top-down approach.

00:14:18.462 --> 00:14:21.032
So I'll sometimes go
the top-down approach

00:14:21.032 --> 00:14:23.762
until I realize, oh, I'm
way out over my skis here.

00:14:23.762 --> 00:14:26.322
I don't actually know how to
have this technology works.

00:14:26.322 --> 00:14:29.372
Let me go back and do a bunch
of bottom-up experiments

00:14:29.372 --> 00:14:33.482
and just work with these
libraries I've never

00:14:33.482 --> 00:14:36.542
worked with or this kind of data
that I've never seen before.

00:14:36.542 --> 00:14:41.642
And once you start to
get a sense of confidence

00:14:41.642 --> 00:14:45.332
about how the problem
is going to be solved,

00:14:45.332 --> 00:14:49.282
then I might switch
over to, OK, let

00:14:49.282 --> 00:14:52.062
me go back to this
approach of top down.

00:14:52.062 --> 00:14:54.752
So the same thing, I
think, for Shiny apps.

00:14:54.752 --> 00:14:56.342
I tend to think top down.

00:14:56.342 --> 00:15:00.442
So if I'm looking at a
dashboard that I want to build

00:15:00.442 --> 00:15:04.072
and it's going to have
these four inputs, I think,

00:15:04.072 --> 00:15:07.642
what data am I going
to need for that?

00:15:07.642 --> 00:15:10.432
Oh, I'm going to have to
do a bunch of aggregations

00:15:10.432 --> 00:15:11.982
in order to get that data.

00:15:11.982 --> 00:15:14.692
Do I also need to access
the unaggregated data

00:15:14.692 --> 00:15:16.312
for other things in the app?

00:15:16.312 --> 00:15:18.862
Well, if so, then I
really want to make sure

00:15:18.862 --> 00:15:20.482
that those are both
things that are

00:15:20.482 --> 00:15:26.422
exposed as not functions in this
case but reactive calculations.

00:15:26.422 --> 00:15:30.832
And then I feel like the
relationship between the inputs

00:15:30.832 --> 00:15:33.802
and the reactive calculations,
I tend not to even think

00:15:33.802 --> 00:15:34.912
about that at all.

00:15:34.912 --> 00:15:36.682
That just sort of happens.

00:15:36.682 --> 00:15:38.579
I call the things that I need.

00:15:38.579 --> 00:15:40.162
And then they end
up depending on them

00:15:40.162 --> 00:15:43.162
unless there's some very
specific reason like, I'm

00:15:43.162 --> 00:15:47.022
going to use this slider input
to inform this calculation,

00:15:47.022 --> 00:15:48.522
but not till this
button is pressed.

00:15:48.522 --> 00:15:49.252
OK, fine.

00:15:49.252 --> 00:15:52.042
Then you use an event for that.

00:15:52.042 --> 00:15:55.342
I can't relate sometimes
when people say to me like,

00:15:55.342 --> 00:15:58.402
oh, there's all these
problems with my reactivity,

00:15:58.402 --> 00:16:01.012
and I have a really
hard time keeping

00:16:01.012 --> 00:16:05.872
track of what inputs are being
referenced by what reactive.

00:16:05.872 --> 00:16:08.752
I'm sort of like, why do
you need to keep track?

00:16:08.752 --> 00:16:13.382
It's not about keeping
track of the global graph.

00:16:13.382 --> 00:16:16.882
So it's really about
each individual reactive

00:16:16.882 --> 00:16:19.612
only accessing the
things that it needs.

00:16:19.612 --> 00:16:21.142
That's the way I approach it.

00:16:21.142 --> 00:16:24.036
[MUSIC PLAYING]

